<section class="hero hero--compact">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
    <h1>Documentation</h1>
    <p class="hero__copy">From your first target to parallel project tests and interactive debugging.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<article class="docs-content panel">
  <section id="start">
    <p class="eyebrow">In five minutes</p>
    <h2>Quick start</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Install and start Browser Testbench once on your machine.</li>
      <li>Check and complete the environment on the <a href="/setup">Overview</a> page.</li>
      <li>Install the client as a development dependency in your project.</li>
      <li>Copy a concrete ID from the <a href="/targets">Test targets</a> page.</li>
      <li>Start your application and use its URL in your test.</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>npm install --global browser-testbench
browser-testbench start</code></pre>
    <p>Run this command in each project that contains automated tests:</p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev browser-testbench</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="remote">
    <p class="eyebrow">Windows, macOS, and Linux</p>
    <h2>Remote Testbench</h2>
    <p>
      Start the machine that owns the browsers and devices with <code>browser-testbench start --remote</code>. Start the
      development computer normally, then use Discover on the Overview page. The first connection asks for the six-digit
      code shown by the remote computer; later explicit connections reuse the saved pairing.
    </p>
    <pre><code>browser-testbench discover
browser-testbench connect &lt;name-or-instance-id&gt;
browser-testbench status
browser-testbench disconnect</code></pre>
    <p>
      If discovery is blocked, use <code>browser-testbench connect --server http://HOST:55808</code>. A remote browser
      cannot reach an application through the development computer's localhost address. Bind the application to the LAN
      and use its explicit LAN URL. Use remote mode only on a trusted private network.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="targets">
    <p class="eyebrow">Convention over Configuration</p>
    <h2>Target-IDs</h2>
    <p>
      Desktop browsers use short IDs such as <code>chrome</code>. Mobile targets include the device and version, for
      example <code>safari-ios-iphone-17-pro-26-5</code>. IDs are generated from the detected environment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="automation">
    <p class="eyebrow">Project-controlled</p>
    <h2>Automated tests</h2>
    <p>Install the Node client in your project:</p>
    <pre><code>npm install --save-dev browser-testbench</code></pre>
    <pre><code>import { RemoteTestbench } from "browser-testbench/client";

const testbench = new RemoteTestbench();
const targets = await testbench.availableTargets(["chrome"]);

await testbench.forEachTarget(targets, { url: "http://127.0.0.1:3000" }, async browser => {
  await browser.fill("#email", "test@example.com");
  await browser.click('button[type="submit"]');
  await browser.waitForText("Welcome");
});</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="debugging">
    <p class="eyebrow">Remote control</p>
    <h2>Interactive debugging</h2>
    <p>Open a long-lived session from the command line or control it through a connected MCP client.</p>
    <pre><code>browser-testbench open --target chrome --url http://127.0.0.1:3000</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="mobile">
    <p class="eyebrow">Physical and virtual devices</p>
    <h2>Mobile devices</h2>
    <p>
      Browser Testbench detects connected devices and shows their concrete target IDs on the Test targets page. Use the
      matching setup below before running the first verification.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="physical-android">
    <p class="eyebrow">USB setup</p>
    <h2>Connect a physical Android device</h2>
    <device-setup-checklist platform="android"></device-setup-checklist>
    <details class="docs-disclosure" id="android-detailed-setup">
      <summary>Show full Android setup guide</summary>
      <div class="docs-disclosure__content">
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="android-tools" name="android-setup">
          <summary>
            Install Android Platform Tools and Appium
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <ol>
              <li>Install Android Studio or the Android SDK Platform Tools on the Testbench computer.</li>
              <li>Install Appium UiAutomator2 under <a href="/setup#environment-setup">Environment setup</a>.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="android-device-connection" name="android-setup">
          <summary>
            Prepare and connect the device
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <ol>
              <li>On the device, enable Developer options by tapping the build number seven times.</li>
              <li>Open Developer options and enable USB debugging.</li>
              <li>Connect and unlock the device with a data-capable USB cable.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="android-debugging" name="android-setup">
          <summary>
            Allow debugging and check Chrome
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <ol>
              <li>Accept the USB debugging authorization prompt on the unlocked device.</li>
              <li>Install or enable Google Chrome on the device.</li>
              <li>
                Open the <a href="/setup">Overview</a> page and confirm that the device is listed as ready under Chrome
                on Android.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="android-first-test" name="android-setup">
          <summary>
            Run the first test
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <p>Open <a href="/targets">Test targets</a> and run the generated physical-device target once.</p>
            <p>
              Connecting, disconnecting, or changing USB authorization updates the interface automatically. Localhost
              URLs are forwarded over USB for the session, so the Android device does not need access to the development
              computer's LAN address.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="physical-ios">
    <p class="eyebrow">USB and Xcode setup</p>
    <h2>Connect a physical iPhone or iPad</h2>
    <device-setup-checklist platform="ios"></device-setup-checklist>
    <details class="docs-disclosure" id="ios-detailed-setup">
      <summary>Show full iPhone and iPad setup guide</summary>
      <div class="docs-disclosure__content">
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="ios-mac-setup" name="ios-setup">
          <summary>
            Install Xcode and the iOS test tools
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <ol>
              <li>Install and open Xcode on the Mac that runs Browser Testbench.</li>
              <li>
                Under Xcode Settings → Accounts, add a free Apple Account or an account belonging to a paid Apple
                Developer team. Browser Testbench never asks for or stores the account credentials.
              </li>
              <li>
                Install Appium XCUITest under <a href="/setup#environment-setup">Environment setup</a> if Browser
                Testbench offers that action.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="ios-device-connection" name="ios-setup">
          <summary>
            Connect and trust the device
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <ol>
              <li>Connect the unlocked iPhone or iPad by USB and accept <strong>Trust This Computer</strong>.</li>
              <li>
                Under Settings → Privacy &amp; Security, enable Developer Mode, restart the device, and confirm it after
                the restart.
              </li>
              <li>
                In Xcode, open <strong>Window → Devices and Simulators</strong>, select the device, and wait until it
                appears without a warning or a <strong>Preparing</strong> status.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="ios-safari-settings" name="ios-setup">
          <summary>
            Enable Safari automation
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <ol>
              <li>Under Settings → Developer, enable <strong>UI Automation</strong>.</li>
              <li>
                Under Settings → Safari → Advanced on iOS 16, or Settings → Apps → Safari → Advanced on newer iOS
                versions, enable <strong>Web Inspector</strong> and <strong>Remote Automation</strong>.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="ios-signing" name="ios-setup">
          <summary>
            Sign and run WebDriverAgent
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <p>
              Appium controls a real iPhone through WebDriverAgent. Apple requires this helper app to have an Apple
              Development certificate and a provisioning profile. A free Apple Account is sufficient.
            </p>
            <h4>Create the signing identity</h4>
            <ol>
              <li>
                Under <a href="/setup#environment-setup">Environment setup</a>, run the WebDriverAgent command shown for
                the iPhone. Keep the opened <code>WebDriverAgent.xcodeproj</code> project open.
              </li>
              <li>
                In Xcode Settings → Accounts, select the team, open <strong>Manage Certificates</strong>, and create an
                <strong>Apple Development</strong> certificate if none exists.
              </li>
              <li>
                Return to the Overview page, select <strong>Check again</strong>, and copy the exact WebDriverAgent
                bundle ID shown for the device.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              If more than one team is installed, set <code>BROWSER_TESTBENCH_IOS_TEAM_ID</code> to the intended
              ten-character team ID and restart Browser Testbench.
            </p>
            <h4>If the certificate is not detected</h4>
            <ol>
              <li>
                In Keychain Access, select <strong>login → My Certificates</strong> and expand Apple Development. A
                private key must appear below it.
              </li>
              <li>
                If the identity remains invalid, install Apple's
                <a href="https://www.apple.com/certificateauthority/AppleWWDRCAG3.cer">WWDR G3 intermediate</a> in the
                login keychain and keep <strong>Use System Defaults</strong>.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <h4>Run WebDriverAgent</h4>
            <ol>
              <li>A paid Developer team can first run the physical target and let Xcode provision automatically.</li>
              <li>
                For a Personal Team or failed automatic provisioning, select
                <strong>WebDriverAgentRunner → Signing &amp; Capabilities</strong>, enable automatic signing, select the
                team, and enter the bundle ID shown by Browser Testbench.
              </li>
              <li>
                Select <strong>Product → Scheme → WebDriverAgentRunner</strong>, select the iPhone under Product →
                Destination, and choose <strong>Product → Test</strong>.
              </li>
              <li>
                If iOS blocks the app, open <strong>Settings → General → VPN &amp; Device Management</strong>, select
                the developer entry, and trust it. The device needs internet access for this confirmation.
              </li>
              <li>Return to <a href="/targets">Test targets</a> and run the physical iPhone target again.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              A free Personal Team profile expires after seven days. Repeat Product → Test after expiry or when an
              XCUITest update replaces WebDriverAgent.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="ios-xcode-compatibility" name="ios-setup">
          <summary>
            Fix “Logic Testing Unavailable” on iOS 16
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <p>
              If the build succeeds but Xcode cannot start WebDriverAgentRunner, signing is already complete. Xcode 26.4
              and 26.6 are known to report this error for physical iOS 16 devices.
            </p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                Download Xcode 26.2 from Apple's
                <a href="https://developer.apple.com/download/all/?q=Xcode%2026.2">More Downloads</a> page.
              </li>
              <li>Extract it, rename it to <code>Xcode-26.2.app</code>, and move it to Applications.</li>
              <li>
                Select it and verify the version:
                <pre><code>sudo xcode-select --switch /Applications/Xcode-26.2.app/Contents/Developer
xcodebuild -version</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>Under Xcode Settings → Components, install the iOS 26.2 platform component if Xcode offers it.</li>
              <li>Return to Test targets and run the physical iPhone target again.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="docs-accordion" id="ios-network-access" name="ios-setup">
          <summary>
            Use the correct application address
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down docs-accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </summary>
          <div class="docs-accordion__content">
            <p>
              The device needs network access to the <strong>tested application</strong>, not to the Testbench UI. USB
              is used separately for device discovery and automation. On the device, <code>localhost</code> refers to
              the device itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              <% if (it.remoteExecution) { %>You must use the IP address of the Testbench that runs the tests. Do not use the IP
              address of the local gateway.<% } else if (it.localNetworkAddress) { %>The current Testbench's detected LAN address is
              <strong><code><%= it.localNetworkAddress %></code></strong
              >. For an application on port 3000, you must use
              <strong><code>http://<%= it.localNetworkAddress %>:3000</code></strong> instead of
              <code>http://localhost:3000</code>.<% } else { %>Browser Testbench could not detect a LAN address
              for this computer. Check its network settings before connecting the device.<% } %>
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="mobile-controls">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interaction</p>
    <h2>Mobile controls</h2>
    <p>Tap, swipe, pinch, portrait, and landscape are available through the Node client and MCP.</p>
    <pre><code>import { BrowserOrientation, PinchDirection, SwipeDirection } from "browser-testbench/client";

await browser.setOrientation(BrowserOrientation.Landscape);
await browser.swipe({ direction: SwipeDirection.Up });
await browser.pinch({ direction: PinchDirection.Out });</code></pre>
  </section>
</article>

<script type="text/x-template" id="device-setup-checklist-template">
  <div class="setup-checklist" :id="elementId" aria-live="polite">
    <div class="setup-checklist__heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Guided setup</p>
        <h3>{{ heading }}</h3>
        <p>{{ intro }}</p>
      </div>
      <button class="button button--secondary" type="button" :disabled="store.loading" @click="refresh">
        <i :class="['fa-solid', store.loading ? 'fa-spinner fa-spin' : 'fa-rotate']" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        {{ store.loading ? 'Checking …' : 'Check again' }}
      </button>
    </div>
    <label class="setup-checklist__device-picker" v-if="physicalDevices.length > 1">
      <span>Device</span>
      <select v-model="selectedDeviceId">
        <option v-for="deviceOption in physicalDevices" :key="deviceOption.id" :value="deviceOption.id">
          {{ deviceOption.name }}{{ deviceOption.platformVersion ? ` · ${deviceOption.platformVersion}` : '' }}
        </option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <div class="setup-checklist__steps" v-if="workbench">
      <details class="setup-checklist__group is-complete" v-if="completedSteps.length">
        <summary>
          <span class="setup-checklist__icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-check" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <strong>{{ completedSteps.length }} {{ completedSteps.length === 1 ? 'step' : 'steps' }} completed</strong>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down setup-checklist__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="setup-checklist__compact-list">
          <div
            class="setup-checklist__compact-step is-complete"
            v-for="step in completedSteps"
            :key="step.id"
            :data-step-id="step.id"
          >
            <i class="fa-solid fa-check" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>{{ stepNumber(step) }}. {{ step.label }}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
      <article
        class="setup-checklist__step setup-checklist__current"
        v-if="currentStep"
        :data-step-id="currentStep.id"
        :class="`is-${status(currentStep)}`"
      >
        <span class="setup-checklist__icon">
          <i :class="['fa-solid', statusIcon(status(currentStep))]" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </span>
        <div class="setup-checklist__copy">
          <div class="setup-checklist__title">
            <strong>{{ stepNumber(currentStep) }}. {{ currentStep.label }}</strong>
            <span>{{ statusLabel(status(currentStep)) }}</span>
          </div>
          <p>{{ currentStep.detail }}</p>
          <label class="setup-checklist__confirmation" v-if="currentStep.manual">
            <input type="checkbox" @change="confirmManualStep($event.target.checked)" />
            <span>I enabled these settings</span>
          </label>
          <details class="setup-checklist__troubleshooting" v-if="currentStep.troubleshooting?.length">
            <summary>If this does not work</summary>
            <ul>
              <li v-for="hint in currentStep.troubleshooting" :key="hint.text">
                {{ hint.text }}
                <a v-if="hint.href" :href="hint.href">{{ hint.linkLabel ?? 'Open help' }}</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </details>
          <a :href="currentStep.href" @click="openInstructions(currentStep.href)">Open instructions</a>
        </div>
      </article>
      <p class="setup-checklist__complete" v-else>
        <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check" aria-hidden="true"></i> {{ completionText }}
      </p>
      <details class="setup-checklist__group" v-if="laterSteps.length">
        <summary>
          <span class="setup-checklist__icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-clock" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <strong>{{ laterSteps.length }} later {{ laterSteps.length === 1 ? 'step' : 'steps' }}</strong>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down setup-checklist__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="setup-checklist__compact-list">
          <div class="setup-checklist__compact-step" v-for="step in laterSteps" :key="step.id" :data-step-id="step.id">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-clock" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>{{ stepNumber(step) }}. {{ step.label }}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</script>
